# {{.Name}}

Generated with the provisional Pluto CLI. Requires Go 1.25.5+. These app-local
primitives will be candidates for framework extraction; no published Pluto API
is imported. Chi is the HTTP router, PostgreSQL/pgx + sqlc the database direction.

```sh
go mod download
go run ./cmd/api
# Another terminal: curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz
```

Authentication fails closed. `/healthz` is public. `/api/features` and newly
scaffolded endpoints require authentication. Inject a real `transport.Authenticator`
in cmd/api; an arbitrary tenant/identity header is never a verified identity.
{{if .Multi}}This app requires verified ActorID AND TenantID. Authenticator verifies the
selected X-Tenant-ID against bearer membership. Operations receive that principal.
Public generated business endpoints are deliberately refused in multi tenancy.{{else}}This app uses single tenancy; no tenant header is used.{{end}}
No demo tokens or cookie sessions are supplied; CSRF protection for cookie auth
is not implemented. Incomplete operations return 501, never fabricated success.

```sh
pluto add endpoint widgets Create --method POST --path /api/widgets \
  --name 'Create widget' --description 'Create a widget after resource checks.' \
  --permission widgets.write --feature widgets-create --feature-client-visible
pluto add endpoint widgets Get --method GET --path '/api/widgets/{id}' \
  --name 'Read widget' --description 'Read a widget in the verified scope.'
pluto add endpoint reports List --method GET --path /api/reports \
  --name 'List reports' --description 'List authorized reports.'
pluto fmt
pluto vet
```

New apps include Docker, mise and fnox files by default. Init can omit each
independently with `--no-docker`, `--no-mise` or `--no-fnox`. Choices are saved;
endpoint additions do not enable omitted presets. Core lint/config/SQL files
are always included and Git hooks remain opt-in.

Commands discover pluto.toml in cwd/parents; saved tenancy/prefix are reused.
`pluto.toml` is project metadata, never runtime settings/secrets. Schema 2 extends
the prototype schema 1 with developer presets and endpoint generation records.
Unknown fields/settings fail; no automatic schema migration is implied.

Authorization imports `github.com/plutoframework/pluto-rbac` at the exact version
in go.mod/go.sum, with no sibling checkout or local replacement. Startup compiles
one immutable policy and injects it into the router and operations.
`internal/access/permissions_gen.go` declares capabilities and named endpoint
permission constants from the manifest. Authored `internal/access/roles.go`
starts empty: adding a permission does not automatically grant it to anyone.

After adding the sample widgets Create endpoint, edit `access.Roles()`:

```go
func Roles() []rbac.Role {
    return []rbac.Role{
        {
            ID:          "member",
            Name:        "Member",
            Description: "Create authorized widgets in the active scope.",
            Permissions: []rbac.PermissionID{WidgetsCreatePermission},
        },
    }
}
```

Role inheritance uses `Inherits: []rbac.RoleID{"member"}` on another named role.
Authored `access.Permissions()` can customize capability names/descriptions or
append capabilities outside HTTP. Unknown grants, parents or cycles fail startup.
The authenticator resolves `Principal.Roles` from verified server-side membership;
never trust an arbitrary role header/claim. A subject can have different roles
in different tenants. Route and direct operation checks use `principal.Can` with
the same injected policy. Unknown roles (even alongside known roles), absent
grants and a missing policy deny permission checks. Scope/ownership checks and
membership freshness remain application responsibilities. The library evaluates
trusted role IDs; this adapter uses the membership snapshot returned by auth.
`pluto fmt` and `pluto vet` validate the resulting app and exercise both boundaries.

Metadata in `[[endpoints]]` is the generation source for CLI-owned permissions, paths,
feature declarations and group/composition registries (`*_gen.go`). Do not edit
these files; vet checks their semantic content against the manifest. Route strings
appear only in paths at runtime. Models, per-endpoint handlers/operations/tests,
config and transport are authored files: the CLI creates them once and refuses
overwrite. Additions regenerate only managed files after checking prior ownership.
Manual catalog additions require taking over the generator contract deliberately.

`internal/handlers/<group>` only decodes input, reads verified context, calls a
consumer-owned operation interface and maps output. Business policies belong in
`internal/operations/<group>`, without HTTP or DB imports. Models are independent
wire types. Path parameters reach the operation via Request.Path, not JSON.
Inject real domain-specific repositories into operations when persistence is needed;
there is no universal database interface, ORM, resource authorization or durable
business audit implementation. Slog is diagnostic output, not durable audit.

Runtime defaults then explicit environment overrides, loaded/validated once and
injected. No implicit .env loading. `.env.example` documents {{.Prefix}} variables.
`FEATURE_<UPPERCASE_KEY>` normalizes dots/hyphens to underscores. Flags default
false; explicit false is preserved using pointers. Client-visible declarations
alone reach authenticated `/api/features`; route gates use the same evaluator.
These startup flags require restart and do not claim OpenFeature compatibility.
Feature visibility does not make an endpoint public or grant permissions.

Responses are `{data:...}` or `{error:{code,message,request_id}}`. JSON decoding
rejects unknown fields, invalid types, trailing JSON, and bodies over 16 KiB.
Known duplicate JSON keys use stdlib last-value semantics; bodies must be JSON
objects. GET/DELETE/HEAD/OPTIONS starters have no body contract. Add explicit
domain validation and resource policies; OpenAPI cannot infer them. The server
sets timeouts/security headers/no-store, negotiates gzip, and logs endpoint,
status, generated request ID and verified actor{{if .Multi}}/tenant{{end}}, never bodies or credentials.

Browser security defaults live in authored `internal/transport/security.go`,
registered globally before recovery/authentication. They apply to successful,
denied, missing, method-error and recovered-panic responses:

- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
  `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` and `Cache-Control: no-store`.
- CSP: `default-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none';
  frame-ancestors 'none'`, suitable for this JSON-only API.
- Permissions Policy disables camera, microphone and geolocation.
- COEP `require-corp`, COOP/CORP `same-origin`, and Origin-Agent-Cluster `?1`.
- DNS prefetch is off; legacy download/cross-domain policies are restricted;
  `X-XSS-Protection: 0` disables obsolete browser XSS filters.

These are inspired by [Fiber Helmet](https://docs.gofiber.io/next/middleware/helmet/),
implemented with net/http and no additional dependency. Adjust the authored
middleware deliberately if serving HTML, interactive documentation or embeddable
resources. CSP controls this API response document, not a separately hosted UI.
CORP limits cross-origin no-cors resource loading; it does not configure CORS.
Cross-origin frontend access still requires a reviewed CORS origin allowlist.
Set HSTS at your HTTPS terminator after choosing the domain policy. The local
HTTP scaffold neither sets HSTS nor trusts forwarded protocol headers; it never
opts a domain into includeSubDomains or preload. Headers do not replace
authentication, authorization, input validation or CSRF defenses for cookie auth.

```sh
go test ./...
go vet ./...
go run ./cmd/openapi -check
go test ./internal/transport -run '^$' -fuzz '^FuzzDecode$' -fuzztime=3s -parallel=2
pluto fmt                 # formatters + wsl fixes + formatters
pluto vet                 # nonmutating; appropriate for CI
pluto vet --staged        # conservative index/working-tree policy; see below
```

Install golangci-lint 2.15.0 from its official releases and put it on PATH, or
use `mise exec golangci-lint@2.15.0 -- pluto vet`. The CLI requires the exact tool
version and fails with guidance if absent. `.golangci.yml` is configurable; its
80-column golines target permits indivisible strings/comments. Vet also enforces
architecture imports (transitively), file sizes, manifest/generated contracts,
OpenAPI freshness, Go vet and tests. Do not compress code to evade size checks.
No formatter or import check can prove every business policy.

Git init runs only outside existing worktrees and never invokes user hooks.
Hook installation is opt-in (`pluto init --hook` or `pluto hook install`). It
refuses existing pre-commit hooks, honors core.hooksPath and never changes Git
config. Installing from a nested project refuses to alter ancestor hooks. An
installed hook invokes `pluto vet --staged` from the resolved project root.
Staged policy: refuse any unstaged, untracked or ignored project files, unresolved index
entries and staged symlinks; verify indexed regular files against their raw Git blob hashes, then vet the
identical working-tree contents. Ignored .env/bin files must be outside the
project for hook use; clean/smudge transformations that differ are refused.
No stash/index rewrite or claim to check a different staged snapshot. Stage all
project files before committing; reinstall the CLI on PATH for the hook. Hooks
are bypassable convenience; CI should run pluto vet.

Database foundation: optional {{.Prefix}}DATABASE_URL opens and pings pgxpool at
startup, then closes on shutdown. No operation uses it yet. db/migrations and
queries provide only a schema metadata probe, not domain persistence. Regenerate:

```sh
sqlc generate  # pin 1.31.1; sqlc.yaml uses pgx/v5, version 2
```

SQL integration requires a real dedicated database; default tests make no SQL
integration claim. No migration runner or production pool tuning is supplied.
{{if .Mise}}
`mise.toml` pins Go, golangci-lint and sqlc and supplies run/fmt/check/openapi
 tasks. Review before `mise trust; mise install`. Install pluto locally first.{{end}}
{{if .Fnox}}
`fnox.toml` has only a nonsecret local log-level value. Run
`fnox exec -- go run ./cmd/api` to scope environment injection to the child.
{{if .Mise}}`mise run run` supplies this command with pinned fnox 1.43.0.{{else}}Install
fnox 1.43.0 on PATH; this project omitted mise.{{end}}
For real secrets, install age, generate a key outside the repo with
`age-keygen -o ~/.config/fnox/age.txt` (do not overwrite an existing key), and add
`[providers.age]`, `type = "age"`, `recipients = ["<your public age recipient>"]`.
Set top-level `default_provider = "age"`, then `fnox set {{.Prefix}}DATABASE_URL --provider age` uses a hidden prompt and
writes ciphertext. Remove the plaintext local value/provider first; keep private keys outside
Git and arrange key access separately in CI. `fnox check --all` validates without
dumping secrets. Never commit .env/private keys or run env dumps for diagnosis.
See https://fnox.jdx.dev/guide/quick-start and provider docs.{{end}}
{{if .Docker}}
`docker compose up --build --wait` starts API + PostgreSQL using local-only
example credentials, loopback published ports 8080/55432, no privileged sockets
or host database mounts. API binds 0.0.0.0 inside its container; authentication
still fails closed. Compose healthchecks verify process liveness and pg_isready;
no domain SQL integration is implied. `docker compose down -v` discards local
state. Docker images use version tags, not immutable digests; review/pin digests
for deployment. Container checks require a running Docker daemon.{{end}}

Prototype boundaries: no identity provider, RBAC role management, durable audit,
domain SQL generator, distributed flags, production deployment, complete OpenAPI
schema inference (recursive types unsupported), or automatic migrations. No
network-free/offline promise: Go modules and optional tools may need downloads.
Generation preflights known conflicts/symlinks and rolls back its own file writes
on ordinary failures, but concurrent writers/process crashes are outside its
transaction guarantee. Keep a backup/version control for authored changes.

Per-endpoint `internal/app/<group>_<action>.go` bindings are authored constructor
choices. Change the operation constructor and this binding together when adding
repositories. Add typed fields to app.Dependencies and construct/inject them in
app.New. Generated group registries accept narrow Operations interfaces and never
choose concrete implementations; additions preserve authored constructor wiring.
